# SELF-GATING. This file was previously protected by its own **/CODEOWNERS
# rule, and that self-gate goes with the rest. What replaces it: the required
# approving review above, plus HIGH_RISK escalation of CODEOWNERS itself, so an
# edit to this register — including one that quietly deletes a path from it, or
# weakens the HIGH_RISK regex — is still read by the strongest reviewer under a
# required review. That is weaker than a human owner and is stated plainly
# rather than papered over.
#
# WHY THIS FILE STILL EXISTS. Every path below was marked high-risk for a real
# reason, and that reason is worth keeping even though the founder is no longer
# the reviewer. This file is now a REGISTER OF SENSITIVE PATHS: prose for a
# human or agent reviewer, not a routing rule. The signal also stays
# load-bearing in machine terms — see ESCALATION below.
#
# Removing the owner is the point: an owned path auto-requests a review, which
# is what put PRs in the founder's queue.
#
# ESCALATION — measured, not asserted.
#
#   .github/scripts/pr-review.sh escalates the reviewer model Sonnet -> Opus
#   (HIGH_RISK) for ALL 14 of the paths registered below. Verified path-by-path
#   on 2026-08-15 by matching each registered pattern against the live regex,
#   not by reading the regex and assuming.
#
#   That coverage is NOT inherited. Before this change it was partial, and two
#   distinct defects were found while measuring it:
#     * paths this register named simply were not in the regex; and
#     * alternatives written with a leading slash — /proto/, /migrations/,
#       /audit, /auth, /esm/ — never matched a REPOSITORY-ROOT path at all,
#       because grep saw no preceding separator. Escalation for those was
#       silently dead. They are now anchored (^|/).
#
#   If you add a path to this register, add it to that regex and re-measure.
#   A register that claims protection it does not have is worse than no
#   register: it is the failure mode this whole change set exists to remove.
#
# --- SENSITIVE PATHS (no owner; read these harder) ---------------------------

# /installer/**
#     Supply-chain critical: cosign verify + S3 fetch. The installer is pulled
#     by unattended `curl | sh` from a CloudFront distribution with no WAF, so
#     it is the one path in this repo where an unreviewed merge reaches users
#     directly, with no `provision` gate in between. Read it as production.

# /v1-surface-contract.toml
#     The canonical mapping of /v1 endpoints to CLI commands. Drift here breaks
#     the customer contract.

# /src/release_channel.rs
#     Picks which artifact a customer install pulls. A wrong channel ships
#     unreleased code to customers.

# /src/license.rs
#     Entitlement enforcement. A weakened check gives away paid capability.

# /src/preflight.rs
#     Guardrails that run before commands hit the platform.

# /Cargo.toml
# /Cargo.lock
#     Dependency manifests — version bumps carry supply-chain and
#     dependency-rot risk.

# /.github/workflows/**
#     Release and CI workflows — supply chain.

# /.github/scripts/**
#     The reviewer harness itself. The thing that judges every other PR should
#     be read with the same care as the thing it judges.

# **/CODEOWNERS
#     This file. A PR that weakens or removes it is a gate-widening attempt
#     regardless of which repo it lives in.

# /scripts/clean-*
# /scripts/purge-*
# /scripts/bulk-delete-*
# /scripts/wipe-*
#     Admin destructive bulk-mutation tooling. Defensive globs: no-op if absent
#     today, still worth reading hard if added later. Irreversible-on-execution.

